my name is Rene Rivera and I'm the vice  president of research and economic  development at Montana State University  when we look at timing in the early  embryo it's very precise so in moving  from one cell to two cells it should  take about eleven plus or minus four  hours the time from two to three cells  should actually be very similar and then  the third and the fourth cells should  appear within one hour of each other and  so what's interesting to me about that  is if those timings don't occur very  precisely throughout development one may  have fewer cells of a particular type  formed so for example if a basal layer  of cells needs to form before neurons  can form if there's too few basal layer  cells then you get fewer neurons and  diseases like Parkinson's disease are  caused by the loss of a population of  cells or a small number of cells and so  you can imagine if you start out life  with fewer neurons of one type you could  lose them more rapidly and get Park in  early onset Parkinson's so we're  interested in that relationship between  how cells form in early development and  how they're lost over time to lead to  visa to disease clearly there's a link  between the number of cells were born  with and our ultimate disease State
 

